  "query_preview": "..\nScattered storms already ongoing and continue to fill \ninto the Tucson Metro which is no surprise given the moisture \nprofile in place today in conjunction with sufficient instability. \nWe'll mainly be monitoring for heavy rainfall this afternoon as \nscattered storms with frequent lightning continue to evolve, but \ncan't rule out strong gusty outflow winds. With very weak flow in \nthe atmosphere, the storms that do form have the potential to dump \nan impactful amount of rain over the same spot resulting in water \nflowing in normally dry washes and low water crossings becoming \ninundated with swift moving water. HREF depicts a slight chance \n(10%) for rainfall totals exceeding 3 inches. If storms do gradually \nevolve into the Tucson Metro proper, we could see urban street \nflooding with ponding of water on roadways and in poor drainage\nareas. This weekend chances for isolated to scattered storms \ncontinues, again with the moisture we have in place the primary \nconcern continues to lean toward heavy rainfall and localized \nflash flooding. \n\nMonday still looks to be a bit more active as a weak disturbance \nmoves across southeast Arizona. Combined with the additional \nmoisture we have in place, any lifting mechanism can help provide \nconfidence in a heavy rainfall event. QPF continues to trend up\nwith WPC placing portions of the forecast area under slight \nrisk for excessive rainfall with the potential for stronger \nstorms producing rainfall rates of 1-2\"/hr leading to the \nincreased risk of localized flash flooding. Time will tell as \nits difficult to time disturbances this far out.",
